{
  "unit": {
    "id": "es-a2-u16",
    "title": "Subjunctive — first steps",
    "subtitle": "Wishes and hopes: quiero que, espero que, ojalá",
    "level": "A2",
    "order": 16,
    "xp_reward": 20
  },
  "grammar": {
    "title": "When one person wants what another should do",
    "intro": "Until now your verbs have stated <b>facts</b>. But when you <b>wish, hope, or wait for</b> someone else to do something, Spanish switches to a different mood: the <b>subjunctive</b>. Don't panic — we only need it in three tiny openers here: <b>quiero que…</b> (I want that…), <b>espero que…</b> (I hope that…), and <b>ojalá (que)…</b> (hopefully…). After these, the verb's vowel <b>flips</b>.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "tag": "la idea",
        "heading": "Why the verb changes",
        "body": "Compare: <b>Hablo</b> español (fact — I speak). But <b>Quiero que hables</b> español (a wish — I want YOU to). The second subject is different from the first, and it's only a wish, so the verb moves to the subjunctive. The trigger is always the little word <b>que</b> after wanting or hoping.",
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "Yo hablo, pero <b>quiero que</b> tú <b>hables</b>.",
            "en": "I speak, but I want you to speak.",
            "note": "two subjects: yo → tú"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "la regla",
        "heading": "The vowel flip",
        "body": "The rule is beautifully simple. <b>-AR</b> verbs take an <b>-e</b>. <b>-ER</b> and <b>-IR</b> verbs take an <b>-a</b>. Opposite of normal:",
        "conj": [
          ["hablar → que yo", "hable"],
          ["hablar → que tú", "hables"],
          ["hablar → que él", "hable"],
          ["comer → que yo", "coma"],
          ["comer → que tú", "comas"],
          ["vivir → que él", "viva"]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "Espero que <b>comas</b> bien.",
            "en": "I hope you eat well.",
            "note": "comer → coma/comas"
          },
          {
            "es": "Quiero que <b>hables</b> con ella.",
            "en": "I want you to speak with her.",
            "note": "hablar → hable/hables"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "ojalá",
        "heading": "Ojalá — the hope word",
        "body": "<b>Ojalá</b> (from Arabic, “God willing”) means <b>hopefully / I hope</b>. It's always followed by the subjunctive, with or without <b>que</b>. A couple of common irregulars: <b>venir → venga</b>, <b>llover → llueva</b>.",
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "<b>Ojalá</b> llueva mañana.",
            "en": "Hopefully it rains tomorrow.",
            "note": "llover → llueva"
          },
          {
            "es": "<b>Espero que</b> vengas a la fiesta.",
            "en": "I hope you come to the party.",
            "note": "venir → venga/vengas"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "takeaways": [
      "After quiero que / espero que / ojalá, use the subjunctive.",
      "The vowel flips: -AR → -e (hable), -ER/-IR → -a (coma, viva).",
      "You need it when the second subject is different: quiero que tú hables.",
      "Ojalá always takes the subjunctive: ojalá llueva, ojalá vengas."
    ],
    "mistakes": [
      {
        "w": "Quiero que hablas español.",
        "r": "Quiero que hables español.",
        "why": "After 'quiero que' use the subjunctive (hables), not the indicative (hablas)."
      },
      {
        "w": "Espero que comes bien.",
        "r": "Espero que comas bien.",
        "why": "comer flips to -a in the subjunctive: comas, not comes."
      },
      {
        "w": "Ojalá viene mañana.",
        "r": "Ojalá venga mañana.",
        "why": "Ojalá always takes the subjunctive: venga, not viene."
      }
    ]
  },
